+## 5. Verified Shared PostgreSQL Environment
+
+This workstation uses an existing shared PostgreSQL installation. It is not a fresh database setup.
+
+### Verified installation
+
+- PostgreSQL client folder: `C:\pgsql\bin`
+- PostgreSQL client executable: `C:\pgsql\bin\psql.exe`
+- PostgreSQL service executable: `C:\pgsql\bin\pg_ctl.exe`
+- PostgreSQL data directory: `C:\pgsql\data`
+- PostgreSQL service port: `5432`
+
+These locations were verified against the active PostgreSQL Windows service on this workstation.
+
+The path `C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\17\bin` is not valid on this workstation and must not be used unless future verification proves that the workstation installation changed.
+
+### Authoritative environment configuration locations
+
+The established main development checkout contains existing environment configuration at:
+
+- `C:\dev\SNS EMR\backend\.env`
+- `C:\dev\SNS EMR\backend\.env.local`
+- `C:\dev\SNS EMR\sns-emr-frontend\.env.local`
+
+These paths identify where the established configuration currently resides. They do not authorize copying, displaying, committing, or modifying secret values.
+
+The current Copilot-managed worktree must not duplicate these files merely to make local services start.
+
+A missing environment file in a worktree is a configuration gap requiring verification. It is not authorization to establish another source of truth.
+
+### Mandatory PostgreSQL rules
+
+- Use the existing shared PostgreSQL service.
+- Never create a second PostgreSQL installation for this repository.
+- Never create an alternate database merely because a worktree lacks local configuration.
+- Never switch to SQLite.
+- Never add a SQLite fallback.
+- Never invent a database name, database user, password, host, port, tenant identifier, or connection string.
+- Never create or overwrite `DATABASE_URL` without verifying the authoritative configuration and receiving explicit authorization.
+- Never duplicate `.env` or `.env.local` files across checkouts or worktrees without explicit authorization.
+- Never expose secrets in repository instructions, terminal summaries, logs, chat responses, commits, pull requests, screenshots, or artifacts.
+- Never hard-code credentials.
+- Never replace the existing database because a startup attempt fails.
+- Never drop, recreate, initialize, reset, truncate, or reseed the shared database merely to start a development worktree.
+- Never run a database mutation during environment discovery.
+- Never change the database port without explicit authorization.
+- Never treat a connection refusal as permission to redesign the environment.
+
+If `psql` is unavailable through `PATH`, use the verified path `C:\pgsql\bin\psql.exe` directly. Do not replace the verified path with a guessed Program Files path.
+
+### Safe read-only verification
+
+Read-only verification may use:
+
+```powershell
+Test-Path 'C:\pgsql\bin'
+Test-Path 'C:\pgsql\bin\psql.exe'
+Test-Path 'C:\pgsql\bin\pg_ctl.exe'
+Test-Path 'C:\pgsql\data'
+& 'C:\pgsql\bin\psql.exe' --version
+& 'C:\pgsql\bin\pg_ctl.exe' --version
+Get-Service -Name '*postgres*'
+```
+
+Do not include passwords directly in commands. Do not print environment secrets. Do not execute database mutations as part of discovery.
